## The Absolute Rule: Default to Refraction

In the TuffEx component family, **every container component featuring a surface layer must strictly default to `refraction`**.

This establishes the visual signature of Tuff. Whether in cards, the flip overlay, modals and drawers, pop-overs, dropdown and context menus or select panels, refraction delivers one unified, refractive aesthetic across the desktop surface, and no component may choose its own.

The component defaults follow the rule: `TxBaseSurface`'s `mode`, `TxCard`'s `background`, the `surface` of `TxFlipOverlay`, `TxModal`, `TxDrawer`, `TxCommandPalette` and the dialogs `TxPopperDialog`, `TxBlowDialog`, `TxBottomDialog` and `TxTouchTip`, and the `panelBackground` of `TxPopover`, `TxTooltip`, `TxDropdownMenu` and `TxContextMenu` (and their submenus), `TxSelect` and `TxSearchSelect` (which all render through `TxBaseAnchor`) are all `refraction`.

core-app's CoreBox lays `refraction` too, but it is a transparent window whose blur comes from the window's own system material (vibrancy on macOS, Mica on Windows). A page's `backdrop-filter` cannot reach that material: it would only sample the surface's own veil again and lay the copy over it until the veil was no longer translucent. So CoreBox's surface sets `systemBackdrop`: it runs no `backdrop-filter`, leaves the blur to the system, and lays only its veil, light and rim (see [BaseSurface](/docs/dev/components/base-surface)).

## Explicit Confirmation Gatekeeper for Mode Switching

If a specialized scenario (a plain white back-office table, say, or a very low-end embedded industrial device) demands switching the material (`mode`, `background`, `surface` or `panelBackground`) from `refraction` to another one (such as `flat`, `mask`, `glass`, `acrylic` or `mica`):
- **Explicit manual user confirmation is mandatory**!
- Neither AI coding agents nor engineers may arbitrarily override the default material.
- This gatekeeper preserves design system integrity across distributed teams and automated workflows.

Temporary swaps at run time are not switches: the `fallbackMode` a surface takes in motion and `autoDetect`'s degradation under power saving or a strained frame budget leave the declared material alone, and the surface returns to it once the condition clears.
